WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:05.770
Mamma Mia: Here We Go Again brings to life
Donna and the Dynamos' backstory in gloriously

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:11.170
fun fashion, but hidden in that prequel story
are many inconsistencies that contradict plot

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:13.480
points and details from the original movie.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:17.439
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here
and today I'm going to reveal and explain

00:00:17.439 --> 00:00:22.159
five ways that Mamma Mia 2 completely ignored
what happened in the first film!

00:00:22.159 --> 00:00:25.359
Obviously, some spoilers ahead if you haven't
seen the movie!

00:00:25.359 --> 00:00:29.570
Apparently, resurrection is a thing in the
Mamma Mia universe because in the original

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:32.960
movie, it's heavily implied that Donna's mother
is already dead.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:35.420
"Somebody up there's got it in for me.

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:36.800
I bet it's my mother."

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.640
"Wasn't she a ray of sunshine."

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.480
However, in the sequel, Donna's mother Ruby
is still very much alive and kicking and makes

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:49.000
a late but show-stopping entrance in the form
of Cher who reunites with her lost love Cienfuegos

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.910
while belting out Fernando.

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:55.610
At the end of the day, Ruby's revival for
Mamma Mia 2 is basically down to writer-director

00:00:55.610 --> 00:01:00.390
Ol Parker who wrote the part specifically
for Cher and saw the chance to make the sequel

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:04.830
"deeper and moodier" by digging into the strained
mother-daughter relationship briefly mentioned

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:06.200
in the first film.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:10.580
As if raising the dead wasn't enough, Cher
is actually only 3 years older in real life

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:13.280
than Meryl Streep who plays her on-screen
daughter!

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:17.920
Still, Ol Parker says Cher "exists separate
from time" and we should just enjoy the show

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:19.950
and not worry our heads about such things.

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:24.750
And I have to say I agree with him as frankly
who doesn't want to see Cher lend her inimitable

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:30.340
style to ABBA classics while dressed in sparkly
tops, white suits, and flowered flares?!

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:34.090
In the first movie, Colin Firth's Harry tells
Sophie that when he was young he bought a

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:37.440
guitar for Donna and it cost him £10 and
his Johnny Rotten T-shirt.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:42.590
Now, in the sequel, Young Harry wears a Johnny
Rotten T-shirt during his Parisian adventures

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:47.510
with Young Donna, but after he's bought the
guitar, he's still wearing that same T-shirt.

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:51.740
Also, how did he ever actually give young
Donna that guitar?

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:55.760
Because in the sequel, although Hugh Skinner's
Harry does follow Donna to Greece, he misses

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:59.650
the ferry to the island and the last time
we see him he's walking away from the dock,

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:01.590
lamenting his fate.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:05.340
Maybe in this new version of the story, young
Harry decided to leave the guitar for Donna

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:09.700
with someone on the island, like Omid Djalli's
Greek Official; or perhaps there's a cut scene

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:11.850
that explains how it ended up with her.

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:15.480
Indeed, there does appear to be a missing
part of the original Mamma Mia timeline in

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:19.411
the sequel because in the first film, Donna's
diary says that "Harry turned up out of the

00:02:19.411 --> 00:02:21.670
blue" so she said she'd show him the island.

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:26.531
Speaking of Donna's diary, in the first movie,
it said she met and slept with Sam first on

00:02:26.531 --> 00:02:32.610
July 17th, followed by Bill on August 4th,
and finally Harry on August 11th.

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:36.000
However, that's not quite the way things happen
in the sequel!

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.700
In Here We Go Again, Donna first meets and
sleeps with a young Harry in Paris, then she

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:44.730
meets but doesn't sleep with a young Bill
in Greece, after which she meets and falls

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:48.930
in love with a young Sam, and finally, she
meets up with Bill again and this time sleeps

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:49.930
with him.

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:53.150
When Lily James was cast in the lead she says
she "was so clear […] that [Young Donna's

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:57.290
sex life] had to be celebrated" but admits
that at first she "was […] worried [the]

00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:01.620
producers would try and dampen it down", so
she was very glad when they "embrace[d] it."

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:05.860
For writer-director Ol Parker, although Donna's
three relationships are relatively short,

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:08.870
it was important to make each one meaningful.

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:13.030
And I think that's the key to why he changed
the order of events from Donna's original

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:14.030
diary.

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:18.280
The idea being that Harry was a charming vacation
fling, then she fell in love with Sam but

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:22.819
he was already engaged and left her, at which
point Bill was there to help her over her

00:03:22.819 --> 00:03:24.340
broken heart.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:28.569
Another miraculous thing about the Mamma Mia
Universe is how people's eye colours seem

00:03:28.569 --> 00:03:30.880
to completely change over the years.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:35.709
So Alexa Davies's young Rosie has blue eyes
compared to Julie Walters' brown eyes, Hugh

00:03:35.709 --> 00:03:40.510
Skinner's eyes as young Harry are much lighter
than Colin Firth's brown eyes, and while Meryl

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:43.780
Streep's Donna has blue eyes, Lily James has
brown eyes.

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:48.010
Although the filmmakers wanted the younger
characters to really remind audiences of their

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:51.780
older counterparts, they were never looking
for a mirror image.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:55.790
When Here We Go Again was casting, Lily James
was actually busy on a publicity tour for

00:03:55.790 --> 00:04:00.920
Baby Driver, but director Ol Parker insisted
the Mamma Mia team wait till she had time

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:02.310
to meet with them.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:05.930
That's because, as the filmmakers have said,
"Lily transcends whether you think she looks

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:07.439
exactly like Meryl.

00:04:07.439 --> 00:04:10.470
[…] She's a tour-de-force!" and "a natural
storyteller in her singing".

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:15.070
For her part, James has said she tried to
pick "a few characteristics, or expressions,

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:19.190
or a physicality that would make [us] hopefully
believe [her] version of Donna would grow

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:20.579
into" Meryl Streep's.

00:04:20.579 --> 00:04:24.590
And Streep herself has given James her seal
of approval, saying she really did capture

00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:26.229
Young Donna's spirit.

00:04:26.229 --> 00:04:29.680
It's perhaps a little odd that James or the
other actors didn't wear coloured contacts

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:33.229
to change their eye colour for the film, so
I wonder if maybe one of them couldn't wear

00:04:33.229 --> 00:04:36.689
them for some reason, or perhaps the filmmakers
decided it wasn't a big deal.

00:04:36.689 --> 00:04:41.139
Either way, their different eye colours totally
pale into insignificance when you consider

00:04:41.139 --> 00:04:45.660
their great performances, especially Lily
James and the way she brings to life a Young

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:46.999
Donna.

00:04:46.999 --> 00:04:50.919
And let's not forget what Donna's three love
interests looked like when they first met

00:04:50.919 --> 00:04:53.919
her in the original movie compared to how
they look in the sequel!

00:04:53.919 --> 00:04:58.439
Gone are young Sam's long locks, headband,
large moustache, and hippie clothes.

00:04:58.439 --> 00:05:02.930
And young Bill has also had a bit of a haircut
and a change of wardrobe from his more flower-power

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:04.300
style too.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:08.330
Young Harry still has a leather jacket, but
gone are his studded leather choker and eyeliner,

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:11.039
and his hairstyle's also less punky.

00:05:11.039 --> 00:05:15.360
Of course, as Colin Firth, Pierce Brosnan,
and Stellan Skarsgard very briefly played

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:19.530
younger versions of themselves in the original
movie, the costume and make-up departments

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:22.879
had a bit of fun with how they made those
actors look different from their present-day

00:05:22.879 --> 00:05:24.280
selves at the time.

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:28.979
However, the filmmakers obviously didn't want
to extend that few-seconds-long joke from

00:05:28.979 --> 00:05:33.570
the original film into a very lengthy joke
in the sequel, and so when it came to clothes

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:37.770
and hairstyles, they went for less exaggerated
looks that would appeal more generally to

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:39.460
modern audiences.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:44.289
By the way, Omid Djalli's Greek official really
did seem very interested in people's changing

00:05:44.289 --> 00:05:49.539
hairstyles, perhaps a bit of meta-commentary
and a wink to fans of the franchise.

00:05:49.539 --> 00:05:53.449
When they made the original Mamma Mia, they
didn't intend to make a sequel, so their creative

00:05:53.449 --> 00:05:57.580
decisions didn't take into account what they
might want to do in a second movie.

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:02.029
Ten years later, they didn't want to be constrained
too much by choices made or throwaway lines

00:06:02.029 --> 00:06:03.029
in the first film.

00:06:03.029 --> 00:06:06.860
For me, the differences are interesting as
they give us some insight into the filmmaker's

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:08.060
thought processes.

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:12.779
I still really love both the movies, they're
made to be fun, enjoyable, and entertaining,

00:06:12.779 --> 00:06:13.779
and they are!

00:06:13.779 --> 00:06:17.780
So, did any of these changes bother you and
did you spot any other differences between

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:19.009
the two movies?

